Headless: tell the game a screen size of the frontend's own

THeadlessSession takes the main window's size either in the main console's cells (setScreenSize()) or in pixels
(setMainWindowSize()), and the later of the two holds: the game is told the grid Mudlet's font fits in it. An app that
draws the main console in a font of its own counts the console's columns and rows itself, and they are what the game
is to be told, while scripts are to see the main window the size the app gave in pixels, which it chooses for the
layout their windows were made for. With setScreenSize() alone, a resize of the app's console replaced that main
window with one made of the console's cells, so scripts read the wrong size and their windows and borders followed it.

This adds setGameScreenSize(columns, rows): from then on, the game is told (NAWS) those columns and rows in place of
the main console's grid, as setScreenSize() tells it (a width below 40 taken as 40, no more than the profile's wrap
width, once a change has held for 0.3s), and the main window's size and the grid stay as they were.

Carried by Mudlet/android's scripts/apply-mudlet-patches.sh until Mudlet/Mudlet has it.

